status: draft           # reserved: draft | evolving | stable | deprecated
created: [ISO-date]
# Stages as DATA, not prose. `to` lists the legal next stages — a graph with
# cycles, not a linear sequence. `to: []` means terminal (by definition, not
# "edges unwritten"). Runs point UP to this definition via their `definition:`
# field, so the definition carries no link back to its runs.
stages:
  - id: [first-stage]
    to: [[second-stage]]
  - id: [second-stage]
    to: [[third-stage], [first-stage]]   # may loop back
  - id: [third-stage]
    to: []                               # terminal
---

# [Process Name] — Definition

A reusable skeleton for [what process this defines]. Runs of this process are
`type: workflow-run` things that name this definition in their structural
`definition:` field and carry a `current_stage` pointing at one of the stage
ids above.

## Stages

**[first-stage]**
- Entry criteria: [when a run enters this stage]
- What it produces: [the deliverable or state change]
- Performer: [who or what executes the stage]
- Gate authority: [who may authorise transition or accept the output]
- Exit / next: [what signals readiness to move, and to where]

**[second-stage]**
- Entry criteria: …
- What it produces: …
- Performer: …
- Gate authority: …
- Exit / next: … (note any rework loop back to [first-stage])

**[third-stage] (terminal)**
- Entry criteria: …
- Performer: …
- Gate authority: …
- What "done" means for a run that reaches here

## Notes

The body is the definition's reason to change — entry/exit criteria, what each
stage means, who or what performs it, and who may authorise its transition or
accept its output. Performer and gate authority are separate declarations; a
machine may execute a human-authorised stage. A run never edits this; it only
advances its own cursor.
Keep instance-specific data OUT of here (that belongs in the run).
